STATUS (vms-d916, 2026-09-15): THE VENEER APPROACH IS RETIRED. THIS FILE NOW
PINS ONLY THE UPSTREAM SOURCE, FOR THE REAL NATIVE PORT (rd vms-9ef).
================================================================================
Baron's ruling (2026-09-15): "you're supposed to be building TCP/IP and OVMX up
so the OpenVMS port of OpenSSH builds and runs. you are not supposed to be hand
hacking a local ssh or hacking up OpenSSH to work with your sloppy LARP."

The earlier contents of this directory implemented exactly the condemned thing:
a -DOVMX_VENEER patch set (sshconnect-veneer.patch, packet-veneer.patch), an
ovmx_ssh_glue.{c,h} transport shim, an ovmx/ sshd wrap (ovmx_ssh_wrap.c +
ovmx_sshd_*.c), and build-openssh.sh / test/run_ssh_build.sh that cross-built a
musl-static ELF against that veneer. All of it has been DELETED. It was a
stand-in, not the port — green-of-a-stand-in counts as nothing (INV-6). The
retired CI job was openssh-static-musl; the retired server scaffold was
src/vmsssh/vmssshd.c and its --wrap adapters (see rd vms-d916).

WHAT SURVIVES, AND WHY THIS FILE STAYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The operator directive that chose OpenSSH is UNCHANGED and is the whole point of
the real port (item vms-22a, parent vms-843; superseded plan item vms-9ef):

    "SSH is THE networking client/server that matters. Do NOT hand-roll SSH —
     crypto and protocol are security-critical. PORT the real OpenSSH (OpenBSD
     upstream + the portable tree, which has an OpenVMS-port lineage via GNV)."

The faithful target (rd vms-9ef) is that upstream tree compiled NATIVELY on the
TCP/IP + OVMX substrate — against the genuine VMS C-RTL (DECC$SHR), the VMS
socket API (DECC$SOCKET / $QIO over BGn:), a VMS PTY, and the genuine
network-login -> LOGINOUT -> DCL path — shipping INSIDE the TCP/IP Services kit
(vms-cb0). NO veneer, NO -DOVMX_VENEER patches, NO wrap/pump/shim: the substrate
is raised until stock OpenSSH's own socket()/tcgetattr()/openpty()/etc. resolve
to real VMS facilities. That work is decomposed under vms-9ef (R1-R4); it does
not yet compile because the substrate is still being built up.

This file is retained because the PIN is what the real port will fetch. The pin
is BY SHA256 — that is the reproducibility invariant, not the file name. Mirror
quirk observed 2026-08-14 (kept for whoever moves the pin): the OpenBSD CDN
serves openssh-10.0p2.tar.gz with BYTES IDENTICAL to openssh-10.0p1.tar.gz (same
SHA256 021a2e70…, inner directory openssh-10.0p1), and the source itself
self-identifies as OpenSSH_10.0p2 (SSH_VERSION in version.h; `ssh -V` prints
"OpenSSH_10.0p2"). So the CONTENT is the security-fixed p2 release; only the
CDN's file name / inner-directory label reads p1. When the real port's fetch
step is written it should pin the exact tarball name that resolves reproducibly
(openssh-10.0p1.tar.gz) with its verified SHA256 and assert the OpenSSH_10.0
banner so the real version is checked, not assumed. To move the pin (genuinely
distinct p2 bytes, or a later 10.x): update Pinned version / Tarball SHA256 /
Inner directory / URLs here and in whatever fetch step the vms-9ef port lands.

LAYOUT — fetch-vendored, NOT a source copy in-tree (same model as libcrypto)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Like third-party/libcrypto (and unlike third-party/tcc, a committed source
copy), OpenSSH is a large, actively-developed tree; committing it wholesale
would bloat the repo. The real port will be a PINNED FETCH: fetch exactly this
tarball, verify it against the SHA256 above (mismatch = hard fail), and build
entirely out-of-tree inside the project's containerized build tooling. Nothing
is installed on the host and no build product ever lands in the repo
(shared-host rule / "ALL deps containerized"). The crypto foundation it links is
the static musl libcrypto already proven by the libcrypto-static-musl CI job
(third-party/libcrypto, LibreSSL) — that job is INDEPENDENT of the retired
veneer and stays.

